# ─── FALSE POSITIVES: Kubernetes registry rules not applicable ─────────────
# KSV032/033/034/035 require images from *.azurecr.io / GCR / ECR domains.
# BC Gov deploys from ghcr.io (GitHub Container Registry) — a controlled,
# trusted registry for this project. These cloud-vendor-specific rules
# do not apply to the BC Gov OpenShift environment.
KSV032
KSV033
KSV034
KSV035
KSV0125

# KSV110: "workloads in the default namespace" — the chart deploys to a
# dedicated OpenShift project namespace, not the Kubernetes default namespace.
KSV110

# ─── FALSE POSITIVES: OpenShift SCC & BC Gov Private Cloud practices ────────
# KSV-0020 & KSV-0021 (runs with UID/GID <= 10000): OpenShift's restricted-v2
# Security Context Constraints (SCC) dynamically allocate a namespace-specific
# high UID/GID range at runtime. Hardcoding runAsUser or runAsGroup in Helm
# manifests causes the admission controller to reject pods when the hardcoded
# ID falls outside the namespace's allocated range (values.yaml already notes
# "do not hard-code runAsUser/fsGroup").
KSV020
KSV021
AVD-KSV-0020
AVD-KSV-0021

# KSV-0011 (CPU not limited): BC Gov Private Cloud intentionally omits
# resources.limits.cpu so containers can burst into unallocated node CPU
# instead of suffering CFS quota throttling under peak load. CPU requests
# (30m backend, 50m frontend) and memory limits are configured.
KSV011
AVD-KSV-0011
